Docker Container

A Docker container is the running version of a Docker image.

Example:

docker run -d -p 8080:80 nginx

This command runs an Nginx container in the background.

Dockerfile for Java Application
FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jdk-alpine

WORKDIR /app

COPY target/*.jar app.jar

EXPOSE 8080

ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]
Explanation
Line	Explanation
FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jdk-alpine	Provides Java 17 base image
WORKDIR /app	Sets working directory inside container
COPY target/*.jar app.jar	Copies Java JAR file into container
EXPOSE 8080	Shows that app runs on port 8080
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]	Starts the Java application
ENTRYPOINT vs CMD
Point	ENTRYPOINT	CMD
Meaning	Fixed main command of container	Default command or default arguments
Use	Used when container should always run one command	Used when default command can be changed
Override	Not easily changed	Easily changed
Example	ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]	CMD ["--server.port=8080"]
Multi-stage Dockerfile for Java
# Stage 1: Build the application
FROM maven:3.9.6-eclipse-temurin-17 AS builder

WORKDIR /app

COPY pom.xml .
COPY src ./src

RUN mvn clean package -DskipTests

# Stage 2: Run the application
FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre-alpine

WORKDIR /app

COPY --from=builder /app/target/*.jar app.jar

EXPOSE 8080

ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]
Why Multi-stage Dockerfile is Used
Reason	Explanation
Smaller image size	Final image contains only required runtime and JAR file
Cleaner image	Source code and Maven cache are not included
Better security	Fewer tools and files reduce security risk
Faster deployment	Small image is faster to push and pull
Production ready	Commonly used in real DevOps projects
Docker Compose

Docker Compose is a tool used to run and manage multiple containers together using one YAML file.

Example services:

Service	Work
Java Backend	Runs Spring Boot application
MySQL Database	Stores application data
Network	Connects containers
Volume	Stores database data permanently
Docker Compose Example for Java and MySQL
version: "3.8"

services:
  mysql:
    image: mysql:8
    container_name: mysql-db
    environment:
      MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD: root
      MYSQL_DATABASE: java_app_db
      MYSQL_USER: raj
      MYSQL_PASSWORD: raj123
    ports:
      - "3306:3306"
    volumes:
      - mysql_data:/var/lib/mysql

  java-app:
    build: .
    container_name: java-backend
    ports:
      - "8080:8080"
    environment:
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL: jdbc:mysql://mysql:3306/java_app_db
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_USERNAME: raj
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD: raj123
    depends_on:
      - mysql

volumes:
  mysql_data:
Docker Network

Docker Network is used to allow containers to communicate with each other.

Example:

A Java backend container can connect to a MySQL container using Docker network.

Docker Network Types
Network Type	Explanation
Bridge	Default network for containers on a single Docker host
Host	Container directly uses host machine network
None	Container has no network access
Overlay	Used for multi-host networking in Docker Swarm
Macvlan	Gives container its own IP address like a real device
Docker Volume

Docker Volume is used to store container data permanently.

It is mainly used for databases like MySQL, MongoDB, and PostgreSQL.

Example:

docker volume create mysql_data
docker run -d \
  --name mysql-db \
  -e MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=root \
  -v mysql_data:/var/lib/mysql \
  mysql:8
Docker Registry

Docker Registry is a storage place where Docker images are stored, uploaded, and downloaded.

Common registries:

Registry	Use
Docker Hub	Public registry for Docker images
Amazon ECR	AWS private container registry
GitHub Container Registry	GitHub registry for images
Harbor	Private enterprise registry
Docker Monitoring and Logs

Docker monitoring means checking container health, CPU, memory, network, and running status.

Docker logs are used to check application output and errors.

Command	Use
docker logs container-name	Shows container logs
docker logs -f container-name	Shows live logs
docker stats	Shows live CPU and memory usage
docker inspect container-name	Shows detailed container information
docker events	Shows real-time Docker events
Docker Scout

Docker Scout is used to check Docker images for security vulnerabilities.

Example:

docker scout quickview image-name

For Nginx:

docker scout quickview nginx:latest

For Java app:

docker scout quickview java-app:latest
Docker Installation on Ubuntu Linux
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y ca-certificates curl gnupg
curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com -o get-docker.sh
sudo sh get-docker.sh
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker
docker --version

Add current user to Docker group:

sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
newgrp docker

Test Docker:

docker run hello-world
10 Important Docker Commands
Command	Use
docker --version	Checks Docker version
docker pull nginx	Downloads image from Docker Hub
docker images	Shows all Docker images
docker build -t my-app .	Builds image from Dockerfile
docker run -d -p 8080:80 --name my-container nginx	Runs container in background
docker ps	Shows running containers
docker ps -a	Shows all containers
docker logs my-container	Shows container logs
docker exec -it my-container sh	Opens shell inside container
docker stop my-container	Stops running container
Docker Compose, Network, and Volume Commands
Command	Use
docker compose up -d	Starts all Compose services in background
docker compose up --build -d	Rebuilds images and starts services
docker compose ps	Shows Compose containers
docker compose logs -f	Shows live Compose logs
docker compose down	Stops and removes Compose containers
docker network ls	Shows all Docker networks
docker network create my-network	Creates Docker network
docker network inspect my-network	Shows network details
docker volume ls	Shows all Docker volumes
docker volume create mysql_data	Creates Docker volume
Real-world DevOps Use Case

